  "hypothesis": {
    "root_cause_service": "fraud-detection",
    "category": "memory_leak",
    "title": "fraud-detection memory leak causes GC pressure and consumer lag",
    "summary": "fraud-detection is experiencing memory pressure with heap usage above 90% after full GC, causing severe GC pauses that slow message processing from 28ms to 521ms p95. The service has no external dependencies, so the slowdown is internal. As a result, it cannot keep up with the Kafka message rate, and consumer lag has grown from 12 to over 3,600 messages. The memory leak is the root cause; the lag and latency are symptoms.",
    "reasoning": "The alert fired on fraud-detection consumer lag exceeding 1000 messages. The lag grew from ~12 to 3,667 messages starting around -20m, coinciding with a latency spike from 28ms to 521ms. The key question is: why is fraud-detection slow? Examining the evidence: (1) Traces show fraud-detection's own spans account for 100% of the added latency (+463ms), with a sample trace spending 467ms in fraud-detection/handle. (2) CPU utilization rose from 14% to 38%, and memory from 50% to 82%. (3) Critically, logs show 300 warnings of \"heap usage above 90% after full GC\" starting at -23m, just before the latency spike. This indicates memory pressure and GC thrashing. (4) The request rate is stable (~3.2 req/s), so this is not a load spike. (5) Fraud-detection has no downstream dependencies in the call graph\u2014it only consumes from Kafka and processes messages internally. The accounting and payment deploys at -12m and -24m are red herrings: accounting is a sibling consumer (not a dependency of fraud-detection), and payment is not on fraud-detection's path at all. The telemetry points to fraud-detection itself suffering from memory pressure causing GC pauses, which slows message processing and creates the lag. This matches a memory_leak pattern: gradual memory growth, high heap after full GC, increasing latency, and consumer lag as a symptom.",
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "supporting_evidence": [
      {
        "evidence_id": "logs:fraud-detection:1",
        "why_it_matters": "300 warnings of heap usage above 90% after full GC starting at -23m directly indicate memory pressure and GC thrashing, the mechanism causing slowdowns."
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "metrics:fraud-detection:p95_latency_ms",
        "why_it_matters": "Latency spiked from 28ms to 521ms starting at -20m, coinciding with memory pressure onset, showing the performance impact of GC pauses."
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "traces:fraud-detection:ae288a75895f",
        "why_it_matters": "A 467ms trace with all time spent in fraud-detection/handle confirms the service itself is slow, not waiting on a dependency."
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "metrics:fraud-detection:consumer_lag",
        "why_it_matters": "Lag grew from 12 to 3,667 messages as processing slowed, demonstrating the symptom of the underlying memory issue."
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "metrics:fraud-detection:memory_utilization",
        "why_it_matters": "Memory climbed from 50% to 82% over 45 minutes, showing gradual growth consistent with a memory leak rather than a sudden spike."
      }
    ],
    "suggested_actions": [
      {
        "title": "Restart fraud-detection to clear leaked memory and restore processing speed",
        "command": "kubectl -n otel-demo rollout restart deployment/fraud-detection",
        "risk": "low",
        "rationale": "Restarting will immediately clear the leaked memory, stop GC thrashing, and allow the consumer to drain the backlog. This is the safest and fastest mitigation."
      },
      {
        "title": "Temporarily increase memory limit to buy time for investigation",
        "command": "kubectl -n otel-demo set resources deployment/fraud-detection --limits=memory=2Gi",
        "risk": "low",
        "rationale": "If a restart alone does not resolve the issue or the leak recurs quickly, raising the memory limit will delay the next GC crisis while engineers investigate the leak's source."
      },
      {
        "title": "Capture a heap dump before restart for root-cause analysis",
        "command": "kubectl -n otel-demo exec deploy/fraud-detection -- jmap -dump:format=b,file=/tmp/heap.hprof 1",
        "risk": "medium",
        "rationale": "Preserving a heap dump will help identify which objects are leaking. Only do this if the service is still running and you have time before the restart."
      }
    ],
    "related_documents": [
      "RB-oom",
      "RB-kafka-lag"
    ],
    "alternatives": [
